"type_conformance.mst — what Smalltalk's types ARE on this VM.

 MACDART hosts Smalltalk on the Dart VM by mapping Smalltalk's kernel classes
 straight onto Dart's built-ins: a SmallInteger IS a Dart int, a Boolean IS a
 Dart bool, nil IS null. There is no wrapper object and no boxing layer — which
 is exactly why the compiler cannot tell the two languages apart, and why the
 loop tax is 1.00x.

 The price is that Dart's semantics show through wherever the two languages
 disagree. This file PINS that boundary. Some probes below assert real Smalltalk
 behaviour; others record a deliberate DEVIATION, so that if the mapping ever
 changes, a test says so instead of a user finding out.

 Deliberately standalone: it uses only literals, the operators the front-end
 maps to Dart IL tokens, identity (==/~~, a StrictCompare), and the inlined
 control flow (ifTrue:/ifFalse:/and:/or:). It needs NO world image loaded, so it
 tests the mapping itself rather than the library on top of it.

 Run:  macdart/st/test/run_conformance.sh"

Object subclass: TypeConformance [

    "--- integers: Dart int (Smi), promoting to Mint/Bigint by itself ---"
    intAdd            [ ^40 + 2 ]
    intMul            [ ^6 * 7 ]
    intIdentity       [ ^42 == 42 ]            "immediates: identity is value"
    intNotIdentity    [ ^42 ~~ 43 ]
    intCompare        [ ^(3 < 4) and: [ 4 >= 4 ] ]
    intBig            [ ^1073741824 * 1073741824 ]   "past Smi -> Mint, exact?"
    intBigIdentity    [ | a b | a := 1073741824 * 1073741824.
                        b := 1073741824 * 1073741824. ^a == b ]
    intMod            [ ^7 \\ 2 ]

    "DIVISION is where the mapping most conspicuously does NOT let Dart through:
     stDivide (cocoa.dart) keeps Smalltalk's EXACT division. 4 / 2 answers the
     int 2, where Dart's own 4 / 2 is the double 2.0.

     An INEXACT division (3 / 2) asks for the world's Fraction class. With the
     world loaded that is what it answers; standalone, stDivide's probe misses
     and it falls back to a plain double. That fallback only became reachable
     when the natives learned to raise a catchable error — see HAZARD below."
    intDivideExact    [ ^4 / 2 ]
    intDivideExact2   [ ^100 / 10 ]
    intDivideInexact  [ ^3 / 2 ]

    "--- booleans: Dart bool ---"
    boolTrue          [ ^true ]
    boolIdentity      [ ^true == true ]
    boolPickFalse     [ ^(1 > 2) ifTrue: [ 'yes' ] ifFalse: [ 'no' ] ]
    boolAnd           [ ^true and: [ false ] ]
    boolOr            [ ^false or: [ true ] ]

    "--- nil: Dart null ---"
    nilValue          [ ^nil ]
    nilIdentity       [ ^nil == nil ]
    nilNotFalse       [ ^nil ~~ false ]        "nil and false are distinct"

    "--- floats: Dart double ---"
    floatAdd          [ ^1.5 + 1.5 ]
    floatMixed        [ ^1 + 1.5 ]             "int + double promotes"
    floatIdentity     [ ^1.5 == 1.5 ]          "boxed doubles: identity?"

    "--- symbols: INTERNED Dart Strings (Symbols::New) ---"
    symbolIdentity    [ ^#foo == #foo ]        "interning must hold"
    symbolEquals      [ ^#foo = #foo ]

    "--- strings: Dart Strings (String::New per literal) ---"
    stringEquals      [ ^'foo' = 'foo' ]
    stringIdentity    [ ^'foo' == 'foo' ]      "two literals, two objects?"

    "Symbol and String are the SAME runtime type here — there is no Symbol ext
     in the dispatch chain. Smalltalk keeps them as different classes."
    symbolVsString    [ ^#foo = 'foo' ]
    symbolIsString    [ ^#foo == 'foo' ]

    "--- characters: one-character Dart Strings ---
     The world file 09_character.mst promises `$a == $a` via a flyweight table,
     but a $a LITERAL compiles to a fresh String::New. This is the probe that
     says whether that promise survives the literal path."
    charEquals        [ ^$a = $a ]
    charIdentity      [ ^$a == $a ]
    charVsString      [ ^$a = 'a' ]            "distinct classes in Smalltalk"

    "--- blocks: Dart closures ---"
    blockValue        [ ^[ :x | x + 1 ] value: 41 ]
    blockCapture      [ | n b | n := 10. b := [ :x | x + n ]. n := 20. ^b value: 1 ]

    "--- collections: Dart List ---"
    arrayLiteral      [ ^#(1 2 3) ]
    byteArrayLiteral  [ ^#[1 2 3] ]

    "HAZARD found by this test on its first run, 2026-07-27 — now FIXED.

     `3 / 2` and `42 become: 43` both ask for something that is not loaded:
     stDivide probes for the world's Fraction class, and become: is looked up
     on a _Smi. Those misses were reported by the natives with Dart_NewApiError,
     which is NOT a catchable Dart exception — it terminates the isolate. Two
     consequences, both of which made a comment in the source untrue:

       - stDivide's `catch (_) { _stFractionPresent = false; }` in cocoa.dart
         was UNREACHABLE. It documents 'a plain double otherwise'; what actually
         happened was that the process died. Every inexact integer division in
         a standalone .mst did this.
       - the same for any stSend/stNew/stInvokeStatic naming something absent:
         a typo was not an error, it was an exit.

     st_natives.cc now raises a catchable error (STThrow) at all eleven sites,
     and both probes below are ordinary checks."

    "--- values the driver inspects from the Dart side ---"
    anInt             [ ^42 ]
    aFloat            [ ^1.5 ]
    aString           [ ^'hello' ]
    aSymbol           [ ^#hello ]
    aChar             [ ^$a ]
    aBool             [ ^true ]
    aBlock            [ ^[ :x | x ] ]
]
